\DOC LOCAL_SQR_P256K1_TAC

\TYPE {LOCAL_SQR_P256K1_TAC : int -> string list -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Advances a symbolic execution goal of the form {A ?- eventually x86 P s} past
an inlined modular-squaring subroutine call, replacing the current state
variable by a fresh later one and recording the mathematical result of the
squaring in the assumptions.

\DESCRIBE
The first argument is an {int} selecting which code block/offset of the
enclosing program the call occupies; the second is a {string list} naming the
squaring's variables, the first string naming the fresh output value and the
second the existing input value. The conclusion predicate is kept, but the
final state argument becomes a fresh state variable at a later program point.
The invariant-style assumptions about the old state — program counter, loaded
bytes, register contents, memory contents, and the accumulated {MAYCHANGE}
relation from the initial state — are deleted and reinstated for the new state,
with the program counter updated to the instruction after the call and the
{MAYCHANGE} relation extended by the memory region written. Two new assumptions
are added: one equating the freshly named output variable with the modular
square of the named input, and one reading that value out of the destination
memory region in the new state. Purely side-condition assumptions
(nonoverlapping facts and earlier arithmetic facts) are retained unchanged.
{
          A ?- eventually x86 P s
     ======================================  LOCAL_SQR_P256K1_TAC n [out; inp]
          A' ?- eventually x86 P s'
}
\noindent where {s'} is the fresh later state and {A'} is {A} with the
state-dependent assumptions re-expressed at {s'} and the new equation for the
squaring result added.

\COMMENTS
In all observed traces, only this single kind of transformation occurs, always
on goals of this symbolic-execution shape.

\ENDDOC
